<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<ui version="4.0">
 <class>Help_DFT</class>
 <widget class="QDialog" name="Help_DFT">
  <property name="geometry">
   <rect>
    <x>0</x>
    <y>0</y>
    <width>640</width>
    <height>480</height>
   </rect>
  </property>
  <property name="windowTitle">
   <string>Dialog</string>
  </property>
  <layout class="QVBoxLayout" name="verticalLayout">
   <item>
    <widget class="QLabel" name="label">
     <property name="text">
      <string>&lt;html&gt;&lt;head/&gt;&lt;body&gt;&lt;p align=&quot;center&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot; font-size:16pt;&quot;&gt;DFT Help&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/body&gt;&lt;/html&gt;</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
   <item>
    <widget class="QTextBrowser" name="textBrowser">
     <property name="html">
      <string>&lt;!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC &quot;-//W3C//DTD HTML 4.0//EN&quot; &quot;http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40/strict.dtd&quot;&gt;
&lt;html&gt;&lt;head&gt;&lt;meta name=&quot;qrichtext&quot; content=&quot;1&quot; /&gt;&lt;meta charset=&quot;utf-8&quot; /&gt;&lt;style type=&quot;text/css&quot;&gt;
p, li { white-space: pre-wrap; }
hr { height: 1px; border-width: 0; }
li.unchecked::marker { content: &quot;\2610&quot;; }
li.checked::marker { content: &quot;\2612&quot;; }
&lt;/style&gt;&lt;/head&gt;&lt;body style=&quot; font-family:'Segoe UI'; font-size:9pt; font-weight:400; font-style:normal;&quot;&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot; font-size:12pt;&quot;&gt;This Tab performs INS simulations (both single crystal and powder) based on provided DFT results, either from the included DFT database or the user's own DFT calculations. The DFT database contains 10,023 from phonondb (see Ref. [4]) and additional ones from the current authors (Cheng23, unpublished). &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; font-size:12pt;&quot;&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot; font-size:12pt;&quot;&gt;Data files required include POSCAR-unitcell, FORCE_SETS or FORCE_CONSTANTS, mesh.conf (where DIM and MP must be specifiied, EIGENVECTORS = .TRUE. should be included, and if FORCE_CONSTANTS is used instead of FORCE_SETS, it must also contain FORCE_CONSTANTS = read), and in some cases BORN (with NAC = .TRUE. in the mesh.conf file). These files are already available if you choose a compound from the database. You may also choose to use your own DFT calculation results, in which case check &amp;quot;Use My DFT Data&amp;quot; and specify the folder containing the above files. Note that the file names and format must be the same as in the database. Please load any entry from the database to check file format when there is an issue using your own DFT results.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; font-size:12pt;&quot;&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot; font-size:12pt;&quot;&gt;When using the provided database, you may search the entries in various ways (selected from the &amp;quot;Type&amp;quot; dropdown list). The search value must be a valid regex (https://www.w3schools.com/python/python_regex.asp). A few examples are given below:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot; font-size:12pt; font-weight:700;&quot;&gt;Na2O&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot; font-size:12pt;&quot;&gt;: will return all entries containing the string, such as TeNa2O3 and GaNa2O4Li3. If &amp;quot;Exact match&amp;quot; is checked, then only Na2O will be found.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot; font-size:12pt;&quot;&gt;If you want to find all compositions containing Na2 and O in any order, use &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot; font-size:12pt; font-weight:700;&quot;&gt;Na2.*O|O.*Na2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot; font-size:12pt;&quot;&gt; for the search.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot; font-size:12pt;&quot;&gt;If you know the Materials Project ID (the number only), you can find the crystal quickly by searching the ID (e.g., 22862 for NaCl)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot; font-size:12pt;&quot;&gt;You may also choose to filter the entries by space group name/number to find materials having a similar structure as what you are interested in.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; font-size:12pt;&quot;&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot; font-size:12pt;&quot;&gt;Once you find the crystal, click on it to choose (the chosen one will be highlighted). Click &amp;quot;Load selected model&amp;quot; to copy the input files.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; font-size:12pt;&quot;&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot; font-size:12pt;&quot;&gt;Step 2 is optional, but it is often convenient to view the crystal structure and check the phonon dispersion and DOS before the INS simulation. In some cases, negative frequencies (imaginary modes) exist, which could indicate issues in modeling or actual instability of the structure. It is essential to be aware of this situation when interpreting the results.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; font-size:12pt;&quot;&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot; font-size:12pt;&quot;&gt;Step 3: Set up INS simulation (Phonopy and OCLIMAX). This step generates a *.params file in the working directory. Detailed instructions can be found in the popup window (the help button within).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; font-size:12pt;&quot;&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot; font-size:12pt;&quot;&gt;Step 4: Run INS simulation. Depending on the size/complexity of the calculation, the time needed to complete this step can vary significantly. Monitor the terminal output for progress. Generally speaking, the run time is determined by the size of the mesh.yaml in the working directory, as well as the requested quality of the calculation (number of  E and Q bins). As a rule of thumb, the size of the mesh.yaml file should be smaller than 1GB (in most cases on the order of 10~100MB), the number of E bins should be less than a few thousand for 1D spectra, and the number of E and Q bins should be less than a few hundred each for 2D spectra. When in doubt, it is recommended to start from a relatively coarse calculation and then gradually increase the quality to achieve convergence without making the run time unacceptably long.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;-qt-paragraph-type:empty; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px; font-size:12pt;&quot;&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot; margin-top:0px; margin-bottom:0px; margin-left:0px; margin-right:0px; -qt-block-indent:0; text-indent:0px;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot; font-size:12pt;&quot;&gt;Step 5: Plot the resulting spectra. For 2D spectra, you may make an interactive cross-section plot where 1D cuts along X and Y will be displayed following the cursor. You may open multiple plots simultaneously. Data files will have a time stamp in their names so they are not overwritten.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/body&gt;&lt;/html&gt;</string>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
  </layout>
 </widget>
 <resources/>
 <connections/>
</ui>
